WEBVTT

00:01.300 --> 00:04.641
♪ It seems today
that all you see ♪

00:04.661 --> 00:08.098
♪ Is violence in movies
and sex on TV ♪

00:08.166 --> 00:11.887
♪ But where are those
good old-fashioned values ♪

00:11.971 --> 00:14.857
♪ On which we used to rely? ♪

00:14.942 --> 00:18.346
♪ Lucky there's a family guy ♪

00:18.397 --> 00:21.734
♪ Lucky there's a man
who positively can do ♪

00:21.801 --> 00:23.319
♪ All the things that make us ♪

00:23.387 --> 00:24.687
♪ Laugh and cry ♪

00:24.739 --> 00:27.213
♪ He's... a...
Fam... ily... Guy! ♪

00:33.267 --> 00:35.686
(crowd yelling and screaming)

00:35.754 --> 00:37.355
There it is,
you guys.

00:37.423 --> 00:39.791
The best roller coaster
in all of Quahog.

00:39.859 --> 00:43.029
The League of Extraordinary
Gentlemen: The Ride.

00:43.096 --> 00:45.098
You know, I think they kind
of figured that movie

00:45.166 --> 00:46.488
was going to turn out
to be bigger than it was.

00:46.513 --> 00:48.057
Oh, come on Lois,
it'll be awesome.

00:48.082 --> 00:49.535
It simulates all the fun of...

00:49.536 --> 00:51.736
of, um...
w-what was it now?

00:51.738 --> 00:54.174
It was like a... like a
tall guy in a black hat

00:54.242 --> 00:55.417
and a cape or something?

00:55.418 --> 00:56.519
Yeah, yeah, and there
was the guy

00:56.527 --> 00:58.419
with the
mask who wore a mask.

00:58.715 --> 01:00.083
And one guy
had an umbrella.

01:00.151 --> 01:02.119
Yeah, and I believe he
opened it at one point.

01:02.186 --> 01:03.755
Yeah, and it
wasn't overly long.

01:03.822 --> 01:05.224
Yeah, not-not
overly long.

01:05.292 --> 01:08.395
Sorry, sir, there's a weight
limit on this coaster.

01:08.462 --> 01:09.996
Oh, really,
what's the limit?

01:10.064 --> 01:11.532
The rule is you can't
look hilarious

01:11.600 --> 01:13.435
on this motor scooter.

01:14.587 --> 01:16.322
(laughing)

01:16.390 --> 01:17.757
I'm sorry, there's
just no way.

01:22.515 --> 01:24.717
Big news out of Six Flags
Columbus today,

01:24.785 --> 01:26.286
as they unveil
what promises to be

01:26.354 --> 01:28.690
the largest roller coaster
in the United States.

01:28.757 --> 01:29.724
That's right, Joyce.

01:29.792 --> 01:31.427
They call it The Holocaust.

01:31.494 --> 01:33.462
The ride has several
Jewish groups up in arms...

01:33.530 --> 01:35.398
as they go over
the first rise.

01:35.466 --> 01:36.400
(chuckles)

01:36.468 --> 01:37.401
No, I'm-I'm kidding.

01:37.469 --> 01:39.004
They're actually pretty upset.

01:39.072 --> 01:40.105
Wow.

01:40.173 --> 01:41.357
I had heard about
the Holocaust,

01:41.425 --> 01:43.176
but I never believed it
till right now.

01:43.243 --> 01:45.095
I have to ride
that ride.

01:45.163 --> 01:46.998
Peter, come on, they're not
going to let you that ride either.

01:47.066 --> 01:48.366
You're overweight.

01:48.434 --> 01:49.802
Yeah, if you want to
ride that roller coaster,

01:49.870 --> 01:51.755
you're going to have
to go on a diet.

01:51.823 --> 01:52.956
A diet, huh?

01:53.024 --> 01:54.057
All right, well,
how's that work?

01:54.125 --> 01:55.443
Well, I suppose
you can start

01:55.511 --> 01:56.643
by keeping a
food journal,

01:56.711 --> 01:57.912
like Quagmire does.

01:57.980 --> 01:59.464
Yeah, in fact,
I got it right here.

01:59.532 --> 02:01.834
"Today I ate peaches...
ginger...

02:01.901 --> 02:03.737
honey... candy... olive..."
You know what?

02:03.804 --> 02:05.306
This is the wrong list.

02:05.374 --> 02:07.676
All right, if that's what it
takes to ride that ride,

02:07.744 --> 02:09.694
I am going on a diet.

02:09.762 --> 02:11.397
And I'm not stopping
till I look as good as my

02:11.465 --> 02:13.650
great Aunt "Legs-Go-All-The-
Way-Up" Griffin.

02:13.718 --> 02:16.988
Hey, baby, do those legs
go all the way up?

02:17.056 --> 02:19.175
Why, yes, they do.

02:19.242 --> 02:21.361
(screaming) Oh, my God!
You're a monster!

02:21.412 --> 02:22.579
Come back.

02:22.663 --> 02:24.415
My legs are shaved
like a little girl.

02:27.520 --> 02:29.789
Hey, what do you
got there, Peter?

02:29.857 --> 02:31.008
Rice cakes.

02:31.076 --> 02:32.393
Never had one, but
somebody told me

02:32.461 --> 02:34.379
this is a really good
way to start your diet.

02:35.916 --> 02:37.717
(screaming)

02:42.108 --> 02:44.093
(coughing and gagging)

02:46.297 --> 02:48.916
(retching)

02:48.984 --> 02:50.368
Who...

02:50.436 --> 02:52.638
who could think of such
a horrible thing?!

02:52.705 --> 02:54.340
(evil laughter)

02:54.408 --> 02:56.359
We know you not like
rice cake.

02:56.427 --> 02:59.063
♪ Ricey, ricey rice,
no one like rice cakes. ♪

02:59.131 --> 03:01.133
(gong chiming)

03:02.736 --> 03:04.353
All right, Peter, the
most important thing

03:04.421 --> 03:06.023
when you're on a diet
is willpower.

03:06.090 --> 03:07.792
Now I'm going to put
this steaming pie

03:07.843 --> 03:09.627
on the windowsill to cool.

03:09.679 --> 03:11.397
Now no matter how many
beckoning fingers

03:11.464 --> 03:13.632
that steam morphs into
or how badly it tries

03:13.683 --> 03:17.103
to sneak its way up your nose,
I want you to resist the urge

03:17.155 --> 03:18.639
to levitate
off the ground

03:18.690 --> 03:19.774
and float towards the pie.

03:19.825 --> 03:21.109
Doesn't seem too hard.

03:21.161 --> 03:23.613
All right, go
stand over there.

03:27.169 --> 03:29.871
(laughing)
No, you don't.

03:31.124 --> 03:34.962
No, no,
you can't go in there.

03:35.013 --> 03:37.164
No, no, stay out of there.
That's my special area!

03:37.216 --> 03:38.333
(screams)

03:38.385 --> 03:40.336
No, stop!

03:40.403 --> 03:41.671
I'm saying no!

03:41.722 --> 03:42.806
(screaming)

03:42.857 --> 03:44.575
What kind of pie is this?!

03:44.643 --> 03:47.312
Peter, it's making me watch!

03:50.684 --> 03:53.304
Peter, I don't understand why
we have to drive all the way

03:53.371 --> 03:55.407
to Ohio to ride a
stupid roller coaster.

03:55.491 --> 03:57.577
Yeah, besides, they're still
not going to let you on.

03:57.644 --> 03:59.329
You haven't actually
lost any weight.

03:59.380 --> 04:02.317
Oh, I think my lithe figure
would suggest otherwise.

04:02.384 --> 04:04.386
A girdle is not a substitute
for weight loss, Peter.

04:04.471 --> 04:05.588
Hey, I'm riding
that roller coaster,

04:05.655 --> 04:07.057
and that's all
there is to it.

04:07.141 --> 04:08.358
Now let's get out of here.

04:08.426 --> 04:09.860
This thing's squishing
all my organs around.

04:09.928 --> 04:12.130
(heart beating)

04:16.153 --> 04:18.889
There it is, kids.
Columbus, Ohio.

04:18.957 --> 04:20.492
Now remember, there
are no rules

04:20.560 --> 04:22.360
about who can wear
stretch pants here,

04:22.429 --> 04:24.330
so you're going to see
some pretty shocking things.

04:24.397 --> 04:27.034
Brian, is this
our vacation?

04:27.085 --> 04:28.336
Yeah.

04:28.403 --> 04:29.903
Oh.

04:29.954 --> 04:31.673
Are we trash?

04:31.740 --> 04:32.841
Kind of.

04:32.909 --> 04:33.876
Oh.

04:40.202 --> 04:42.187
I'm sorry, sir. You're too fat
to ride the coaster.

04:42.255 --> 04:43.723
Aw, darn it.

04:43.791 --> 04:45.592
Yeah, why don't you go back
to your pond, hippo?

04:45.659 --> 04:46.660
(laughing)

04:46.728 --> 04:48.028
How about me, sir?

04:48.096 --> 04:49.698
Am I too fat to ride?

04:49.765 --> 04:50.899
No, you don't seem to be.

04:50.967 --> 04:52.501
(laughing crazily)

04:53.771 --> 04:55.606
Well, you got
to hand it to him.

04:55.673 --> 04:57.508
He had a dream
and he made it happen.

04:57.576 --> 04:58.710
That's what
the Wright Brothers did.

04:58.777 --> 05:01.548
So, how's your boyfriend,
Orville, doing?

05:01.616 --> 05:03.083
Ex-boyfriend,
if you don't mind.

05:03.150 --> 05:04.885
And who cares?

05:04.953 --> 05:06.587
He's probably off
in a barn somewhere

05:06.656 --> 05:08.624
working on a contraption
with that weird brother of his.

05:08.691 --> 05:09.809
Ew.

05:09.893 --> 05:11.728
(both laughing)

05:11.796 --> 05:14.348
ORVILLE:
Ha-ha, bitch.

05:14.433 --> 05:16.267
You're so pretty. I miss you.

05:18.238 --> 05:19.740
(gears grinding)

05:25.248 --> 05:28.318
(crashing, people screaming)

05:28.386 --> 05:30.888
PETER: Well, this has given me
a lot to think about.

05:30.956 --> 05:33.425
WOMAN (screaming):
My son is dead!

05:37.131 --> 05:40.017
We are going to have
to send that family money.

05:40.101 --> 05:41.169
I know we are.

05:41.220 --> 05:42.520
Mom, I'm hungry.

05:42.605 --> 05:44.173
Can we stop
and get some food?

05:44.224 --> 05:45.941
Well, I guess it is
close to dinnertime.

05:46.009 --> 05:47.009
Chris, Stewie,
you hungry?

05:48.980 --> 05:51.182
(yawning)

05:51.233 --> 05:52.700
What are you looking at?

05:52.785 --> 05:54.153
You going to propose?

05:54.204 --> 05:55.754
What?
Yeah, exactly.

05:55.822 --> 05:57.157
"What?" Eyes front.

05:57.208 --> 05:58.375
(engine sputtering)

05:58.459 --> 05:59.393
What the hell?!

06:04.501 --> 06:05.885
(loud hissing)

06:05.969 --> 06:08.305
Looks like it's the radiator.

06:08.373 --> 06:10.825
Well, we're just going to have
to find a local mechanic.

06:10.893 --> 06:12.511
On a Friday evening?

06:12.562 --> 06:13.946
I don't think we're going to
have any luck till Monday.

06:14.014 --> 06:16.550
Besides, probably not a lot
of mechanics around here.

06:21.091 --> 06:23.510
So, we're stuck here?

06:23.577 --> 06:25.196
Hey, Lois, you see
that barn out there?

06:25.263 --> 06:26.630
You know who
it was built by?

06:26.698 --> 06:28.233
♪ By... Mennonites. ♪

06:28.301 --> 06:31.204
Well, I guess we better see if
there's a hotel or something.

06:31.271 --> 06:33.507
I can't believe we're
stuck in Amish country.

06:33.575 --> 06:35.009
Yeah, I thought
something was "a-mish."

06:46.734 --> 06:48.064
Hey, uh, excuse me, fella?

06:48.065 --> 06:49.632
Yeah, we're looking for a room.

06:49.700 --> 06:51.535
Are ye indeed, outsiders?

06:51.603 --> 06:54.272
We do not see many of
your folk in these parts,

06:54.340 --> 06:56.776
but we welcome all of God's
children in their hour of need.

06:56.844 --> 06:59.363
Well, actually, I think we need
it for more than an hour.

06:59.431 --> 07:01.533
Or do we, right, Lois?

07:01.599 --> 07:03.935
I-I do not last very long.

07:08.024 --> 07:10.460
Ugh, creepy. What's this?

07:10.528 --> 07:12.113
It's an Amish doll.

07:12.181 --> 07:13.464
They're not allowed
to have faces.

07:13.532 --> 07:14.833
Has something to
do with vanity.

07:14.901 --> 07:16.452
Looks like a cross-dressing
Cobra Commander.

07:16.520 --> 07:17.987
Who?

07:18.055 --> 07:19.590
Cobra Commander.
He's the head of Cobra.

07:19.658 --> 07:21.325
The bad guys from G.I. Joe.

07:21.393 --> 07:23.095
The guys who oddly
shout the name

07:23.162 --> 07:26.165
of their terrorist organization
as they're attacking.

07:26.233 --> 07:28.502
Guys, we're under attack.
By who?

07:28.570 --> 07:29.470
I don't know.

07:34.177 --> 07:36.797
Cobra!

07:36.864 --> 07:37.999
Oh, it's Cobra.

07:38.066 --> 07:39.567
Oh, of course.

07:39.635 --> 07:41.720
Look, they have the pictures
of the snakes on their planes.

07:41.788 --> 07:43.323
Duke, what have I been
saying for years?

07:43.390 --> 07:45.426
What makes a good
terrorist organization?

07:45.494 --> 07:47.428
Brand recognition.
Brand recognition.

07:47.496 --> 07:49.481
Mom, I can't stay here.

07:49.549 --> 07:52.084
There's no electricity, no
telephones, it's horrible.

07:52.152 --> 07:53.387
Excuse me.

07:53.455 --> 07:56.040
I assure you,
our community is quite pleasant.

07:56.125 --> 07:58.026
Perhaps I could
show thee around.

07:58.094 --> 07:59.695
(amorously):
Okay.

07:59.763 --> 08:00.997
My name is Eli.

08:01.065 --> 08:02.632
My parents run the inn.

08:02.700 --> 08:05.369
If there is anything we can do
to make your room more plain,

08:05.437 --> 08:06.638
do not be afraid to ask.

08:06.706 --> 08:07.990
Ah, we'll be all right.

08:08.057 --> 08:09.692
A hotel in Amish country
can't be any worse

08:09.760 --> 08:11.195
than staying in a youth hostel.

08:11.263 --> 08:12.546
Hi, I'd like a room.

08:12.614 --> 08:14.449
Uh, preferably one
where I have to sleep

08:14.517 --> 08:16.251
with my bag
taped to my body.

08:16.320 --> 08:18.820
We have one where some Italian
backpackers have laid out

08:18.889 --> 08:20.656
every item of clothing
they own on all of the beds.

08:20.724 --> 08:21.925
Oh, perfect.

08:21.993 --> 08:23.944
Will my bunkmates be
constantly talking about me

08:24.012 --> 08:25.046
in a foreign language?

08:25.114 --> 08:26.782
Yes, they will, sir.
Oh, great.

08:26.850 --> 08:28.651
Uh, also, I'm going to have
some mail delivered here,

08:28.718 --> 08:31.154
and I want it to sit in
that box for four years.

08:31.222 --> 08:32.924
Oh, and what street
is the bathroom on?

08:36.212 --> 08:37.897
So, what do you do
for fun around here?

08:37.982 --> 08:40.734
We do chores and we watch
other people do chores.

08:40.819 --> 08:42.052
Sounds kind of boring.

08:42.103 --> 08:44.406
Alas, there are times
when I feel as if

08:44.490 --> 08:46.025
there is much of life
I am missing.

08:46.076 --> 08:48.562
Yeah, it'd be tough
having no technology.

08:48.613 --> 08:50.081
I don't think I could
live without my iPhone.

08:50.165 --> 08:51.732
You have an iPhone?

08:51.784 --> 08:53.235
Yeah. You've heard of it?

08:53.286 --> 08:55.455
Yes, but only because
people sometimes search

08:55.523 --> 08:57.240
for Amish farms
on their iPhones.

08:57.291 --> 08:58.459
Here comes one now.

09:08.791 --> 09:10.876
Thanks for a fun evening, Eli.

09:10.944 --> 09:13.379
And-and thanks
for being so nice to me.

09:13.448 --> 09:15.274
I think you are the
most fascinating girl

09:15.299 --> 09:16.500
I have ever met, Meg.

09:18.989 --> 09:22.025
Here. I want you
to have this.

09:22.093 --> 09:23.526
Your iPhone?

09:23.594 --> 09:24.862
Yeah.

09:24.930 --> 09:27.950
I made you a playlist
of songs that I like.

09:28.017 --> 09:29.702
Who is this I am hearing?

09:29.770 --> 09:30.937
It's Avril Lavigne.

09:31.005 --> 09:32.356
Why does she shout at me?

09:32.424 --> 09:33.724
I don't know.

09:33.792 --> 09:35.993
Most of my music I just
get pressured to buy.

09:36.061 --> 09:37.562
Thank you for
this gift, Meg.

09:44.472 --> 09:47.426
Eli! What are you doing
with that outsider?

09:47.494 --> 09:49.428
Her name is Meg, Father.

09:49.496 --> 09:50.997
And I was just showing her
our community.

09:51.082 --> 09:52.450
You stay away from her.

09:52.517 --> 09:53.684
She will try
to corrupt you.

09:53.752 --> 09:55.187
That is not true, Father.

09:55.254 --> 09:56.355
Meg is wonderful.

09:56.422 --> 09:58.308
She is an outsider.

09:58.375 --> 10:00.159
She does not follow
the one true path.

10:00.228 --> 10:02.212
You are not to see
this girl again.

10:02.280 --> 10:04.149
Do you understand?
I forbid it.

10:04.216 --> 10:06.469
(dejected sigh)
Yes, sir.

10:06.536 --> 10:09.506
Now come home at once.

10:09.574 --> 10:12.527
And, as for you,

10:12.611 --> 10:15.899
I suggest you stay away
from my son, you harlot.

10:17.084 --> 10:20.588
(sobbing)

10:22.692 --> 10:25.361
Hey, there, young one,
why so sad?

10:25.429 --> 10:27.965
It's just that I really like
this boy, but his dad...

10:28.033 --> 10:29.034
Oh, look out.

10:29.101 --> 10:30.168
(loud smacks)
(screaming)

10:30.236 --> 10:31.470
Sorry, there.

10:31.538 --> 10:32.755
Didn't realize
how long it'd been

10:32.839 --> 10:34.775
since Ol' Dumper
did his business.

10:34.842 --> 10:36.443
He'll be done soon enough.

10:36.511 --> 10:38.446
(smacking continues)
Oh, maybe not.

10:38.514 --> 10:40.582
Doesn't sound
like pooping, does it?

10:40.650 --> 10:42.285
Sounds more like someone

10:42.353 --> 10:44.388
dropping sandwiches
off a rooftop.

10:44.456 --> 10:46.090
Watch out below, right?

10:46.158 --> 10:47.726
Look like Hacky Sacks.

10:47.794 --> 10:49.929
You'll be sorry
if you kick 'em, though.

10:49.997 --> 10:52.732
All righty, well he seems
to be all done now.

10:52.800 --> 10:53.900
You have a good evening...

10:53.967 --> 10:55.235
(smacking resumes)
Oh, my goodness.

10:55.303 --> 10:57.037
He must have gotten into
the chicken feed again.

10:57.105 --> 10:58.640
You'd think they'd know

10:58.708 --> 11:01.544
it wasn't in their diet,
but this is how they find out.

11:01.611 --> 11:03.313
You may want
to close your mouth.

11:09.251 --> 11:12.989
(sobbing)

11:13.057 --> 11:14.774
Meg, what's wrong?

11:14.842 --> 11:16.477
Eli's father,
Ezekiel, said

11:16.545 --> 11:19.131
that I can't see him
anymore! (sobs)

11:19.198 --> 11:21.768
He said I was trying
to corrupt his son!

11:21.835 --> 11:23.186
He did, did he?

11:23.254 --> 11:24.371
Well, that does it!

11:24.439 --> 11:26.575
We are gonna teach
this town how to dance!

11:26.642 --> 11:28.728
Oh, Meg, I'm so sorry.

11:28.795 --> 11:30.796
I'd try to talk to Eli's father
for you, honey,

11:30.864 --> 11:32.599
but I don't know
if it's my place.

11:32.667 --> 11:33.985
Ezekiel wouldn't listen
to you anyway, Lois.

11:34.053 --> 11:35.253
You're a woman.

11:35.321 --> 11:37.256
These people live in
a patriarchal society.

11:37.324 --> 11:39.793
The only member of our family
whose word would carry

11:39.860 --> 11:41.529
any weight
would be Meg's father.

11:41.597 --> 11:44.266
Dad, will you talk
to Ezekiel?

11:44.334 --> 11:45.550
Will you tell him

11:45.618 --> 11:47.120
I'm not trying to
corrupt his son?

11:47.187 --> 11:48.154
Please?

11:48.222 --> 11:49.523
All right, I'll do it.

11:49.591 --> 11:51.292
But don't expect
any miracles.

11:51.359 --> 11:52.460
I'm not good
with confrontation.

11:52.528 --> 11:53.862
Like when I got stuck

11:53.929 --> 11:55.331
behind that giraffe
at the ballgame.

11:55.399 --> 11:56.932
Yeah! Nice hit!

11:57.000 --> 11:58.435
Frozen rope!
Awesome!

11:58.503 --> 11:59.469
What just happened?

11:59.537 --> 12:01.039
Ground rule double! Whoo!

12:01.106 --> 12:02.273
I couldn't
even see it.

12:02.342 --> 12:03.542
Yeah, it was something.

12:03.610 --> 12:05.345
Ducks on a pond!
Let's bring 'em in!

12:05.413 --> 12:06.680
Could you please
sit down?

12:06.747 --> 12:08.315
I am sitting down.
You sit up.

12:08.382 --> 12:09.416
I am sitting up,

12:09.483 --> 12:11.152
but I still can't see
a thing.

12:11.219 --> 12:12.386
Yeah, sounds
like a "you" problem.

12:12.454 --> 12:13.555
Let's go, Sox!

12:15.993 --> 12:17.193
Before we commence

12:17.261 --> 12:20.732
with Samuel Fisher's
barn raising, let us pray.

12:20.800 --> 12:22.133
Dear Amish Lord,

12:22.201 --> 12:25.604
thou looketh sternly down
upon us thine flock,

12:25.672 --> 12:27.808
even though we did not
do anything wrong

12:27.875 --> 12:31.413
and have been doing
chores like (bleep) crazy,

12:31.481 --> 12:34.417
please make us humble,
and deliver us more hardship,

12:34.485 --> 12:36.954
that we may get thick,
calloused hands,

12:37.022 --> 12:38.924
much larger
than other people's.

12:38.991 --> 12:41.728
And grant that we become dull,
like Eric Bana,

12:41.795 --> 12:43.396
who we have never seen,

12:43.464 --> 12:44.898
but are just going
by reputation

12:44.966 --> 12:46.835
because it is your will.

12:46.902 --> 12:49.238
We solemnly believe
that although humans

12:49.306 --> 12:51.607
have been around
for a million years,

12:51.676 --> 12:53.210
you feel strongly
that they had

12:53.277 --> 12:55.146
just the right amount
of technology

12:55.214 --> 12:58.016
between 1835 and 1850;

12:58.084 --> 13:00.253
not too little,
not too much.

13:00.321 --> 13:02.256
Please deliver us
from Thomas Edison,

13:02.324 --> 13:04.760
the worst human being
who ever lived.

13:04.827 --> 13:06.695
And protect us
from those who laugh

13:06.763 --> 13:08.765
at our buggies or our hats

13:08.832 --> 13:10.734
and deliver us
from mustaches.

13:10.802 --> 13:12.787
Amen.

13:12.855 --> 13:14.005
Hey, uh, Ezekiel?

13:14.090 --> 13:15.891
You must be Mr. Griffin.

13:15.959 --> 13:18.962
Look, I-I know you guys are busy
with your barn raising and all,

13:19.030 --> 13:21.199
but, um, you know,
I just wanted to tell you

13:21.266 --> 13:23.235
how much my daughter Meg loves
your boy Eli.

13:23.303 --> 13:25.788
I promise you, she ain't trying
to corrupt him or nothing.

13:25.855 --> 13:27.473
Mr. Griffin,
with all due respect,

13:27.524 --> 13:29.025
we live in two
different worlds.

13:29.110 --> 13:32.229
I must do what is best
for my son... and I have.

13:32.297 --> 13:33.965
Hey, how do we know
what's gonna happen?

13:34.033 --> 13:35.968
For all we know,
Meg might want to Amish herself.

13:36.036 --> 13:38.271
I had not considered this.

13:38.338 --> 13:41.042
I was verily preoccupied
with Eli's corruption.

13:41.110 --> 13:43.061
Plus, it is the time
of the month

13:43.129 --> 13:44.697
when Eli's mother
is on the burlap.

13:44.765 --> 13:46.800
So whaddaya say,
let the kids give it a shot?

13:46.868 --> 13:48.668
Perhaps, Mr. Griffin.
Perhaps.

13:48.736 --> 13:49.870
Good.

13:49.922 --> 13:51.389
Now on another note,
I notice you don't have

13:51.458 --> 13:53.258
a damn lick of music
in this town.

13:53.326 --> 13:55.361
We do not believe in
such ostentations.

13:55.429 --> 13:58.566
Well, I am about
to change your mind.

13:58.633 --> 14:01.470
Introducing rock 'n' roll!

14:01.537 --> 14:02.805
♪ Whoo! ♪

14:02.873 --> 14:07.762
♪ I'm on a highway
to hell! ♪

14:07.830 --> 14:10.633
♪ Highway to hell! ♪

14:10.700 --> 14:12.084
♪ I'm on the high... ♪

14:12.152 --> 14:14.571
You are banished from
our community at once!

14:14.639 --> 14:16.641
And take your whore
daughter with you!

14:20.080 --> 14:21.347
How did it go, Dad?

14:21.415 --> 14:22.683
Very well, Meg.

14:22.751 --> 14:24.751
My recollection is that
it went extremely well.

14:24.803 --> 14:27.539
Really?! Oh, my God, Dad,
that's fantastic!

14:27.607 --> 14:29.141
So I-I can see Eli again?

14:29.209 --> 14:30.343
EZEKIEL:
Griffins!

14:31.946 --> 14:34.399
(crowd clamoring)

14:34.450 --> 14:36.302
What's going on here?

14:36.369 --> 14:37.937
Your family must leave
our community

14:38.004 --> 14:40.172
and return to the outside world.

14:40.240 --> 14:41.341
Dad, what's happening?

14:41.409 --> 14:42.843
I thought you talked to him.

14:42.910 --> 14:45.746
Sir, please, whatever your
quarrel is with my husband,

14:45.814 --> 14:47.282
I'm sure it's
probably justified,

14:47.350 --> 14:50.587
but I'm begging you, don't
punish the children for it.

14:50.654 --> 14:51.856
They're innocent in all this.

14:51.923 --> 14:53.157
Innocent, are they?

14:53.225 --> 14:55.510
I found this
in Eli's corn hole!

14:55.578 --> 14:57.046
Well, I... His what?

14:57.114 --> 14:59.783
A compartment in Eli's room
where he stores his corn.

14:59.851 --> 15:02.070
Oh. Well, okay,
whatever you say.

15:02.137 --> 15:03.371
Look, Ezekiel, we would love

15:03.439 --> 15:04.673
to go back to the city,
believe me.

15:04.741 --> 15:06.475
I-I haven't had a drink
in three days.

15:06.543 --> 15:08.011
But our car broke down.

15:08.079 --> 15:10.147
We-we can't go anywhere
until it gets fixed.

15:10.215 --> 15:12.868
I have arranged
to have that taken care of.

15:17.292 --> 15:19.460
This ride's gonna be
so frustrating.

15:19.527 --> 15:20.929
I know.
These horses are

15:20.997 --> 15:23.199
so slow.
Sexy.

15:23.267 --> 15:24.600
Sexy.

15:27.740 --> 15:30.007
STEWIE: Good-bye,
Abraham Lincoln people.

15:31.695 --> 15:34.397
(sobbing)

15:34.482 --> 15:35.566
I'm sorry, Meg.

15:35.651 --> 15:38.737
Eli was the best boy
I'll ever meet.

15:38.821 --> 15:41.124
And now I'll never
see him again.

15:41.191 --> 15:43.911
ELI:
Yes, you will, Meg.

15:43.995 --> 15:44.962
(gasps) Eli.

15:45.029 --> 15:46.881
Eli? What are you doing here?

15:46.949 --> 15:48.701
I could not let you
leave without me, Meg.

15:48.752 --> 15:49.919
I love you.

15:50.003 --> 15:52.873
I love you, too.

15:54.593 --> 15:56.594
Hey, Lois, watch me lay rubber
in front of these losers.

15:56.679 --> 15:57.679
Hyah!

16:00.767 --> 16:03.153
Yeah! Choke on that, nerds.

16:08.544 --> 16:10.579
Eli, I agree
that your father was being

16:10.647 --> 16:11.815
somewhat unreasonable,

16:11.883 --> 16:14.117
but I don't think
running away is the answer.

16:14.185 --> 16:16.187
I know, Mrs. Griffin.
And I am sorry.

16:16.255 --> 16:18.090
But I just could
not bear the thought

16:18.158 --> 16:19.609
of never seeing Meg again.

16:19.677 --> 16:21.427
You ever seen one
of these, Eli?

16:21.495 --> 16:23.063
It's a Coleco
football game.

16:23.131 --> 16:24.431
Pretty mind-
blowing, huh?

16:24.499 --> 16:25.600
You know what?

16:25.668 --> 16:27.887
This is garbage to us.

16:27.971 --> 16:29.071
(clattering)

16:29.139 --> 16:30.491
What the hell is that?

16:36.449 --> 16:38.318
Holy crap! Who did this?

16:38.386 --> 16:40.103
It must have been my father.

16:40.171 --> 16:41.873
This is how
the Amish declare a feud.

16:41.940 --> 16:43.392
But I thought they
were nonviolent.

16:43.459 --> 16:44.610
We are,

16:44.677 --> 16:47.146
but Peter must have
really pissed off my dad.

16:47.214 --> 16:48.582
Hey, who raked the leaves?

16:48.650 --> 16:49.750
And look,
somebody mowed the lawn.

16:49.818 --> 16:51.219
I was supposed to do that.

16:51.304 --> 16:53.489
Ha-ha! I have done your chores

16:53.557 --> 16:55.758
to rob you of the joy
of doing chores.

16:55.826 --> 16:57.027
You bastard.

16:58.848 --> 17:00.348
Peter, we don't have
a choice.

17:00.416 --> 17:01.817
We've gotta bring
Eli back.

17:01.885 --> 17:03.252
Oh, no, we don't.

17:03.320 --> 17:04.788
What are you talking about?

17:04.856 --> 17:07.091
We-we can't be part of a feud
with the Amish.

17:07.159 --> 17:08.559
I didn't start
this thing, Lois,

17:08.627 --> 17:10.729
but I'm gonna finish it.

17:13.550 --> 17:15.818
Joe, go round up Quagmire

17:15.886 --> 17:17.855
and all the modern-day
technology you can find.

17:17.923 --> 17:20.158
We are going to war.

17:20.226 --> 17:21.994
Oh. Oh, hey, Bonnie.

17:22.062 --> 17:24.030
Is Joe there?

17:24.097 --> 17:26.500
Well, can I leave
a message for him?

17:26.568 --> 17:30.239
Yeah, it's what I said before
about the-the war thing.

17:35.364 --> 17:36.530
(tires screeching)

17:42.373 --> 17:43.474
(beeping)

17:50.083 --> 17:51.518
All right, men,
this is it.

17:51.586 --> 17:54.188
Technology versus
old-timey wooden stuff.

17:54.256 --> 17:55.724
Let's hit 'em hard.

17:55.792 --> 17:56.725
What, again, was
the nature

17:56.793 --> 17:58.127
of your beef
with them?

17:58.194 --> 17:59.429
I don't know.
Something about

17:59.497 --> 18:00.580
a roller coaster.
Let's go.

18:11.781 --> 18:13.448
Apples... fire!

18:17.321 --> 18:18.372
(all gasping, yelling)

18:18.456 --> 18:19.540
Ah! Damn it!
Ow! God!

18:19.625 --> 18:21.542
Quilters... advance!

18:27.602 --> 18:29.804
Oh! Oh! Ooh! Ooh! Ooh!
Ooh! Oh! Ooh! Oh!

18:29.855 --> 18:31.171
I bet this hurts so much!

18:32.642 --> 18:33.642
(screams)

18:33.693 --> 18:35.845
Ow! Damn it! I'm bleeding!

18:35.896 --> 18:37.448
Uhp, now it's an AIDS quilt.

18:39.952 --> 18:41.169
(gasps)

18:45.760 --> 18:47.795
Suck on this,
you furry little weirdoes!

19:13.230 --> 19:14.282
Enough of this crap.

19:15.401 --> 19:16.235
Ezekiel.

19:19.473 --> 19:23.027
Call off your boys and let's you
and me finish this man-to-man.

19:23.095 --> 19:25.547
Indeed.
Prepare for a goodly beating.

19:27.084 --> 19:28.518
(tires screech)

19:30.722 --> 19:32.474
Dad, stop.
Leave him alone.

19:32.558 --> 19:34.443
Father, what are you doing?

19:34.511 --> 19:36.011
I am going to
kill that man.

19:36.079 --> 19:37.781
That man
is my girlfriend, Meg.

19:37.849 --> 19:39.199
(Peter laughs)

19:39.267 --> 19:41.619
This is against everything
our people stand for.

19:41.703 --> 19:43.505
Will you listen to me
for one moment?

19:43.573 --> 19:46.475
You were so worried
about these outsiders

19:46.543 --> 19:48.611
coming into our community
and corrupting me.

19:48.679 --> 19:50.381
But look at yourself.

19:50.449 --> 19:53.018
You're the one who's allowed
yourself to be corrupted.

19:53.086 --> 19:56.222
God in heaven...

19:56.290 --> 19:57.690
you are right.

19:57.758 --> 20:00.260
I am deeply ashamed.

20:00.311 --> 20:02.547
I only wanted to protect you.

20:02.615 --> 20:04.083
I know you did, Father.

20:04.151 --> 20:07.053
PETER:
Kiss! Kiss! Kiss! Kiss!

20:07.121 --> 20:10.758
(sighs)
Son, it is your life to live.

20:10.827 --> 20:13.028
I must accept
the life you choose.

20:16.384 --> 20:18.987
I choose to stay here.

20:19.055 --> 20:20.372
What?!
What?!

20:20.439 --> 20:23.176
Meg, you are the
most beautiful woman

20:23.243 --> 20:24.511
I will ever know.

20:24.579 --> 20:26.631
You have shown me
so many wondrous things

20:26.682 --> 20:29.802
that have opened my eyes
to the world beyond my home.

20:29.853 --> 20:32.789
But this is where I belong.

20:32.857 --> 20:34.492
I'm sorry
if this upsets you, Meg.

20:34.560 --> 20:36.829
(voice breaking):
I love you, Eli.

20:36.896 --> 20:38.463
I'll always love you.

20:38.531 --> 20:40.800
If this is what you
have to do,

20:40.868 --> 20:43.137
then all I can say is...

20:43.205 --> 20:45.106
I'll never forget you.

20:48.445 --> 20:51.882
(sighs) I am sorry there has been
conflict between us.

20:51.950 --> 20:53.585
Allow me to make amends.

20:53.652 --> 20:57.056
Please help yourself
to anything in my corn hole.

20:57.123 --> 20:58.291
Hey, don't worry
about it.

20:58.359 --> 20:59.759
We can still be pals.

20:59.827 --> 21:01.729
And I know just
the place for us

21:01.797 --> 21:03.898
to go and blow off
all this steam.

21:07.946 --> 21:12.514
♪ We went dancing
across the USA ♪

21:12.515 --> 21:20.371
♪ On that crazy King's Highway ♪

21:20.372 --> 21:24.310
♪ Too much passion ♪

21:24.378 --> 21:28.215
♪ Too much play ♪

21:28.283 --> 21:33.646
♪ We went dancing, dancing,
dancing across the USA ♪

21:33.671 --> 21:36.792
♪ We went dancing, dancing,
dancing across the USA ♪

21:36.817 --> 21:40.375
♪ We went dancing, dancing,
dancing across the USA ♪

21:40.400 --> 21:42.984
♪ We went dancing, dancing,
dancing across the USA ♪

21:42.985 --> 22:43.985
Thank you for watching "Family Guy" on
www.FOX-fan.ru